/*! \page coin_render_experimental Experimental WebGPU renderer

  \brief Opt-in rendering path for evaluating WebGPU without changing the
  public ABI of Coin 4 or replacing its Open Inventor traversal model.

  The experimental renderer is built as a separate
  <tt>CoinRender</tt> library. Applications opt in with
  <tt>COIN_BUILD_RENDER</tt> and continue to own their Coin scene graphs and,
  for window targets, the native window and event loop.

  \section coin_wgpu_inventor Open Inventor contract

  CoinRenderAction is an SoAction. It traverses through SoCallbackAction,
  observes SoState through elements and bundles, and obtains fallback
  geometry through the usual primitive callbacks. It does not introduce a
  second scene graph or a WebGPU-specific node hierarchy.

  \section coin_wgpu_flow Frame flow

  A frame has four implementation responsibilities. These names describe
  ownership of decisions; they do not impose source directory layers.

  - <b>Core</b> validates and transforms Coin-native captured data and
    connector results. It does not traverse or submit GPU work.
  - <b>Shell</b> turns outcomes into diagnostics, logs and profiling text.
  - <b>Infra</b> owns the private WebGPU connector, device, queue, resources,
    submission, presentation and readback.
  - <b>Wiring</b> is the CoinRenderAction orchestration that connects Coin
    traversal, frame planning, a render target and result publication.

  The concrete flow is:
  \code
  Coin scene graph -> CoinRenderAction traversal -> CoinRenderFramePlan
                   -> private backend connector -> WebGPU target
                   -> readback/presentation -> application
  \endcode

  CoinRenderFramePlan and the C ABI used by the Rust bridge are private implementation
  contracts. Only headers under <tt>experimental/include</tt> form the
  experimental source API.

  Status translation and C++ phase-line formatting have a private Shell owner.
  Public callers still observe only the existing status enums and diagnostic
  strings. <tt>COIN_RENDER_TRACE_PHASES</tt> enables stable, whitespace-delimited
  <tt>key=value</tt> records prefixed by <tt>COIN_RENDER_PHASE</tt>.

  Indexed geometry follows a capture/transform boundary. Wiring reads
  SoCallbackAction, SoState and elements, while a traversal-free Core validates
  and transforms Coin-native indexed arrays. Concave and general polygons
  continue through Coin's established tessellation fallback.

  \section coin_wgpu_reuse Typed frame reuse

  Consecutive private FramePlans have one conservative relationship:
  <tt>reuse</tt>, <tt>camera_patch</tt>, <tt>resource_rebuild</tt>,
  <tt>full_rebuild</tt> or <tt>unknown</tt>. Core classifies already captured
  Coin-native data; Wiring keeps traversal and cache ownership; Infra applies
  only the update it can prove against its currently owned base revision.

  A camera patch preserves packed geometry, indices, draws, materials,
  textures and samplers, and regenerates render-state matrices and uniforms.
  It is accepted only if its base revision still matches the FFI packer. This
  matters when actions or targets are reused in different orders: a stale
  hint becomes a full pack rather than observing unrelated storage. Opaque
  GPU texture tokens and unversioned plans remain <tt>unknown</tt>.

  An unchanged scene-root identifier still skips traversal. A restricted
  camera overlay also avoids geometry traversal: the root must be an exact
  SoSeparator with one direct first-child perspective or orthographic camera,
  and all remaining nodes must be from a small exact-type, camera-independent
  allowlist. Only BASE_COLOR draws without fog or opaque GPU texture tokens
  qualify. A priority-zero SoNodeSensor on the root proves that every change
  since the last successful frame originated in a camera field; structural,
  geometry, unknown and mixed notifications force full Coin traversal. A
  changed viewport or background also invalidates the cached root. Wiring
  captures the new Coin view volume, Core overlays only camera-dependent
  snapshots, and Infra still validates the owned base revision before packing.
  The overlay is transactional: Core checks the new camera and existing
  render-state references, then changes only the cached camera, its dependent
  state and the revision in place. Wiring rolls those fields back if frame
  execution fails. Geometry arrays keep their addresses. The private target
  may reuse its full profile validation only when the camera patch names its
  currently validated base; a stale base or resize forces full validation.
  The Rust connector retains its own ABI-boundary validation, including on
  each new camera revision unless its device owns the matching validated
  opaque, untextured scene. For that restricted case, the private frame view
  names the camera base revision and Rust reads immutable geometry only from
  its own snapshot. It checks camera-derived states before reuse; a stale
  base, different device generation, texture or unsupported state takes the
  full validation path. The retained CPU payload is capped at 32 MiB; RTT
  uses the full path. Device destruction/loss drops the snapshot. This
  changes only the private bridge protocol, not the public Coin 4 ABI.
  This is not a replacement for the Open Inventor traversal model. With phase
  tracing enabled, <tt>plan_reuse</tt> and <tt>pack_mode</tt> expose the path.

  Offscreen targets return color and depth by default. The experimental
  CoinRenderTarget::setDepthReadbackEnabled() output policy can request color
  only while retaining GPU depth testing. The Rust connector then omits depth
  staging and mapping; the target's depth accessor is empty and asynchronous
  tickets record no depth output. Switching policy invalidates synchronous
  readback until another successful render. Window and direct RTT targets do
  not accept this output policy. The GL comparison benchmark uses color only
  by default and reports color+depth WebGPU runs separately.

  \section coin_wgpu_targets Targets and readback

  CoinRenderTarget creates either an offscreen target or a target backed by
  a native surface descriptor. Synchronous rendering includes readback when
  pixels are requested. CoinRenderAction::applyAsync() separates submission
  from polling, but does not by itself guarantee a lower total elapsed time.
  CoinRenderReadbackTicket remains valid independently of the originating action
  and target; successful polling consumes it.

  Offscreen readback staging buffers are reused only after their map has
  completed, the mapped view has been dropped, and the buffer has been
  unmapped. The pool is owned by one Rust device generation and retains at
  most two free buffers per size, 16 free buffers and 16 MiB free. Buffers
  above 8 MiB are not cached. In-flight asynchronous tickets retain their
  buffers independently; cancellation does not release a buffer still used
  by the GPU. Color-only readback copies mapped rows directly to its output
  buffer; color plus depth retains transactional publication. This resource
  reuse does not change synchronous frame identity or promise an asynchronous
  latency improvement. <tt>staging_color_reused</tt> is available in opt-in Rust
  phase traces.

  On the synchronous Rust offscreen path,
  <tt>COIN_WGPU_ATTACHMENT_CACHE=1</tt> retains one completed RGBA8/depth
  attachment pair per device and dimensions, up to 32 MiB. Async tickets
  and direct RTT do not borrow this pair. The cached pair is reused only
  after the previous synchronous readback completes; a different size or
  device generation cannot reuse it. <tt>attachments_reused</tt> in the
  phase trace reports cache hits. The cache is opt-in.

  When color staging rows are contiguous, readback uses a single CPU copy;
  padded rows still copy per row. <tt>COIN_WGPU_FORCE_ROW_COPY=1</tt> is a
  diagnostic A/B switch. CoinRenderTarget::borrowRGBA() exposes the last
  successful synchronous pixels without a second C++ copy. The caller must
  finish using the pointer before another render, resize, output-policy
  change or target destruction. This borrowed view belongs only to the
  experimental API; readbackRGBA() retains its copying contract.

  The benchmark's <tt>--async-depth 2</tt> mode pipelines two existing
  readback tickets and measures submit, end-to-end latency and throughput
  separately. It preserves frame order and does not change the synchronous
  API or promise a throughput benefit.

  \section coin_wgpu_profile Measurement contract

  Performance comparisons should use Release builds and report traversal and
  CoinRenderFramePlan construction, resource preparation, submit, GPU execution and
  readback separately. Report median, p95, visual error and memory, and compare
  against OpenGL on the same adapter and workload. Direct render-to-texture
  and asynchronous readback are opt-in mechanisms, not performance claims.

  <tt>COIN_RENDER_TRACE_PHASES=1</tt> emits <tt>rust_cpu_detail</tt> records
  separating attachment creation, draw encode, scene snapshot, staging,
  submit, map request, GPU wait, map reception, color/depth CPU copy,
  publication and recycling. Its <tt>total_ms</tt> includes all listed CPU
  spans for one frame. With <tt>COIN_WGPU_GPU_TIMESTAMPS=1</tt> as well,
  synchronous offscreen frames request GPU timestamp queries if supported
  and emit <tt>rust_gpu render_ms</tt> and <tt>copy_ms</tt>; otherwise the
  status says unsupported. Timestamp probing is opt-in and perturbs timing.
  The CPU poll wait is not a GPU execution timestamp. GPU copy includes
  both color and depth if depth readback was requested.

  \section coin_wgpu_bgfx BGFX evaluation connector

  The optional <tt>COIN_RENDER_BACKEND=BGFX</tt> branch reuses the Coin traversal
  and private CoinRenderFramePlan but executes a deliberately smaller profile through
  BGFX/Vulkan by default, or BGFX/OpenGL when
  <tt>COIN_BGFX_RENDERER=opengl</tt>, on Linux offscreen or Xlib windows.
  Core lowers validated Coin-native
  snapshots without BGFX headers; Infra owns the global BGFX instance,
  renderer-specific SPIR-V/GLSL shader program,
  GPU resources, offscreen RGBA readback and direct window presentation;
  Wiring selects the backend.
  This does not make BGFX a WebGPU implementation or extend Coin 4's stable
  public ABI. The connector currently accepts only indexed, opaque,
  untextured BASE_COLOR triangles in a full target viewport, with depth
  testing but no depth readback. Window targets do not read pixels back.
  Unsupported Open Inventor state is rejected
  explicitly. BGFX uses a process-wide API thread and this evaluation permits
  only one prepared target per process. A concurrent prepare returns
  UNSUPPORTED without disturbing the owner; releasing the owner on its API
  thread permits a serial handoff. The version 2 capability query initializes a
  temporary 1x1 offscreen target and releases it before returning. It reports
  the actual renderer, GPU IDs, normalized format support, MRT, independent
  blend, compute and timestamp support. If the singleton is already owned, the
  query reports COIN_RENDER_PROBE_BUSY with the backend diagnostic instead of
  declaring the GPU unavailable. For an Xlib target, this proves the GPU and
  renderer only; real presentation still requires preparing the caller's native
  surface. OpenGL readback rows are normalized to the same target RGBA
  orientation.
  Core preserves Coin's floating-point clear color, and Infra passes it to
  BGFX's float clear palette without early RGBA8 quantization.
  Test each renderer in a separate process because BGFX initialization is
  process-global. The Coin 4 public ABI is unchanged. See
  <tt>docs/coin-bgfx-evaluation.md</tt> for build, limitations and the Release/GL gate.

  A validated camera-only CoinRenderFramePlan revision updates draw transforms while
  retaining BGFX geometry buffers; other changes use full lowering.
  <tt>COIN_BGFX_DISABLE_CAMERA_PATCH=1</tt> is an A/B diagnostic switch.
  <tt>COIN_RENDER_TRACE_PHASES=1</tt> reports camera patch, upload and readback
  phases. Adding <tt>COIN_WGPU_GPU_TIMESTAMPS=1</tt> probes the BGFX GPU
  frame timestamp with extra frame advancement; do not use those perturbed
  runs as the Release performance comparison.

  \section coin_wgpu_limits Experimental status

  The module is intentionally separate from <tt>libCoin</tt>. Its profile and
  capabilities can change while it is experimental. Query
  coin_render_query_capabilities() before relying on optional
  features, and treat CoinRenderAction::UNSUPPORTED as a normal conservative
  refusal when the captured Open Inventor state cannot be represented safely.

  \see CoinRenderAction
  \see CoinRenderTarget
  \see CoinRenderSceneManager
  \see coin_render_query_capabilities
*/
